{"reference":"Ezekiel 24","book":"Ezekiel","chapter":24,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading Ezekiel 24 within the book","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"This chapter ties its dated announcement to Babylon's approach against Jerusalem and presents Ezekiel's restricted mourning after his wife's death as a sign concerning the sanctuary and the people's coming bereavement. Within the book's narrated horizon, it belongs to the movement from deportation through Jerusalem's siege and destruction toward later restoration and nation oracles. A practical outline groups chapters 4–24 together, though that outline does not settle the book's exact chronology or composition. The book maintains a sustained first-person Ezekielian voice while also showing features compatible with collection, coordination, repetition, and later textual development; this remains an interpretive, disputed way of describing how the received book took shape.","verse_refs":["Ezekiel 24:1-27","Ezekiel 24:15-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ezekiel_024_dced1c33812b9169cfba9a6b"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}